2. RESEARCH INTEGRITY REQUIREMENTS:

a) ACADEMIC USE: Any academic publication, thesis, dissertation, or research 
   paper that uses this software or its concepts must:
   - Cite the academic authors (Cavalcanti & Figueiredo, 2026)
   - Acknowledge the specific relativistic physics implementations
   - Reference the benchmark methodology if used

b) COMMERCIAL USE: Commercial applications may use this software but must:
   - Include the copyright notice in documentation
   - Acknowledge the academic research foundation
   - Not claim original authorship of the relativistic physics algorithms

c) DERIVATIVE RESEARCH: Any derivative academic work must:
   - Clearly distinguish new contributions from existing work
   - Maintain proper attribution
   - Share improvements with the research community when published

===============================================================================
RESEARCH DATA AND METHODOLOGY PROTECTION
===============================================================================

3. BENCHMARK DATA PROTECTION:
The benchmark methodology, test configurations, cross-platform performance 
measurements, and measurement techniques developed for this research are 
protected academic contributions. Any use of similar benchmark approaches 
should cite this work appropriately.

4. PHYSICS IMPLEMENTATION PROTECTION:
The specific GPU compute implementations of Schwarzschild and Kerr metrics, 
numerical integration methods (Euler and RK4 schemes), and relativistic 
multi-body ray marching algorithms represent novel academic contributions 
and should be cited when used or adapted.
